

🧩 Importing from C (DFDL): Supported Features — Short Notes

🔹 1. Purpose

IBM DFDL importer automatically converts C structs/typedefs → DFDL schema
👉 Taki manually DFDL likhne ki zarurat na pade.


---

🔹 2. Example

C Struct:

struct Employee {
    int id;
    char name[10];
    float salary;
};

Importer Output (DFDL Schema):

<xs:element name="Employee">
  <xs:complexType>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="id" type="xsd:int" dfdl:length="4" dfdl:lengthUnits="bytes"/>
      <xs:element name="name" type="xsd:string" dfdl:length="10" dfdl:lengthUnits="bytes"/>
      <xs:element name="salary" type="xsd:float" dfdl:length="4" dfdl:lengthUnits="bytes"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>
</xs:element>


---

🔹 3. C → XML Schema Mapping

C Data Type	XML Schema Type	Notes

char	xsd:string (len=1)	Single character
char[10]	xsd:string (len=10)	Fixed-length string
int	xsd:int	4 bytes
unsigned int	xsd:unsignedInt	4 bytes
short	xsd:short	2 bytes
long long	xsd:long	8 bytes
float	xsd:float	4 bytes
double	xsd:double	8 bytes
enum	xsd:int/short	Based on size option
pointer	xsd:hexBinary	Address as binary



---

🔹 4. C → DFDL Physical Mapping

C Data Type	DFDL Properties

char	dfdl:length="1" bytes
char[10]	dfdl:length="10" bytes"
int	dfdl:length="4" bytes"
short	dfdl:length="2" bytes"
long long	dfdl:length="8" bytes"
float	dfdl:length="4" bytes"
double	dfdl:length="8" bytes"
pointer	dfdl:length=4/8 bytes (32/64-bit)
enum	Based on chosen size (1/2/4 bytes)



---

🔹 5. Array Example

struct Marks {
   int scores[3];
};

→

<xs:element name="scores" type="xsd:int"
            maxOccurs="3"
            dfdl:length="4" dfdl:lengthUnits="bytes"/>


---

🔹 6. Limitations

packed keyword not supported

128-bit long unsupported

Recursive structs fail

Only ANSI C, not C++

Enum size must be manually set



---

🔹 7. Use Case

Legacy systems (C-based binaries, sensor data, billing files) →
Importer auto-creates DFDL schema for parsing/serializing in IIB/ACE Flows.




⚠️ Importing from C (DFDL): Restrictions — Short Notes

🔹 1. Typedef

Anonymous struct typedef not supported ❌

typedef struct { int id; } MyStruct; // Error



---

🔹 2. Empty Structs / Unions

Empty struct or union import → creates duplicate message names (conflict).



---

🔹 3. Preprocessor Macros

#if, #ifdef, #else, #elif not handled properly.

🔧 Fix: remove macros manually before importing.



---

🔹 4. Hexadecimal Constants

Array sizes like int a[0x80]; not supported.

🔧 Convert to decimal → int a[128];.



---

🔹 5. Trigraphs

Old C trigraphs (e.g., ??= for #) unsupported.

🔧 Replace with normal characters.



---

🔹 6. Syntax Strictness

Header file must be syntactically correct for target platform.

Else → unpredictable results / toolkit crash.



---

🔹 7. 64-bit Integer Issue

On 64-bit systems, int may map to xsd:int (only 32-bit).

🔧 Manually change to xsd:long.



---

🔹 8. Anonymous Struct inside Struct

Nested anonymous struct causes import failure.

🔧 Give each struct a name/tag.



---

🔹 9. Enum Issues

Enums with negative values fail to import.

🔧 Remove or make all values positive.



---

🔹 10. Name Alterations

Names in UPPERCASE or starting with _ auto-modified.
e.g. _NAME → Name.



---

🔹 11. Multi-Dimensional Char Arrays

e.g. char[10][3][6] → schema error or wrong maxLength.

🔧 Manually verify generated schema.



---

🔹 12. C/C++ Reserved Keywords

If struct name = keyword (e.g. compl), import silently fails.

🔧 Rename identifier before import.



---

🔹 13. Bit Fields

int flag:1; → imported as xsd:hexBinary.

🔧 Edit manually: set type + alignment in DFDL editor.



---

🔹 14. Octal Constants

int a[080]; misinterpreted by importer.

🔧 Replace with correct decimal.



---

✅ Summary

Importer works best with simple C structs (int, float, char[]).
Avoid complex constructs → macros, typedefs, nested/anonymous structs, enums with negatives, bit fields, or multi-d arrays.

